\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

On 30 July 2026, attackers began emptying Bitcoin wallets whose seeds had been generated on
\coldcard{} hardware devices. One wave emptied 1{,}196 addresses in 41 minutes, and published
totals for the campaign range from 1{,}367 bitcoin (\$88.6 million) to about \$130 million,
depending on the cut-off date~\cite{trm2026coldcard,cbc2026coldcard,thn2026coldcard,galaxy2026coldcard}.
No device was touched. During a March 2021 migration to a new cryptography library, a symbol
collision linked seed generation to MicroPython's non-cryptographic \emph{Yasmarang} generator
instead of the hardware source, and a build guard that tested whether a macro was defined,
rather than whether it was enabled, never fired~\cite{coinkite2026backgrounder,block2026coldcard}.
How much entropy the affected seeds kept is disputed. The vendor's preliminary estimate is a
search space of about 40 bits on the Mk2 and Mk3 and about 72 bits on the Mk4, Mk5, and
Q~\cite{coinkite2026backgrounder}. Block's analysis bounds the later models at \bits{32}
distinguishable output streams once the fallback state and call history are known, inside a raw
ceiling near \bits{73}, and states that exploitability was not confirmed
empirically~\cite{block2026coldcard}. Every estimate is far below the intended 128 bits, and for
five years nothing in the devices' behavior revealed the fault.

The incident is new in scale, not in kind.
Randomness is the substrate on which every other cryptographic guarantee rests: key generation,
signature nonces, initialization vectors, session identifiers, challenge values, and padding
all consume it, and all of them fail catastrophically when it is predictable. It is also
uniquely treacherous to defend, because a broken generator produces output that is
indistinguishable from a working one until the moment it is exploited. The historical record
bears this out. The Netscape browser's SSL keys were predicted from the process ID and clock in
1996~\cite{goldberg1996netscape}. A 2008 Debian packaging change reduced OpenSSL key entropy to
15 bits, so that every affected key could be enumerated~\cite{yilek2009debian}. Internet-wide
scans in 2012 recovered the private keys of more than a hundred thousand TLS and SSH hosts that had
generated keys with too little boot-time entropy~\cite{heninger2012mining,lenstra2012ron}. A
2013 Android \texttt{SecureRandom} defect made Bitcoin wallets reuse ECDSA nonces, which thieves
exploited to sweep them~\cite{michaelis2013randomly}. The \dualec{} generator carried a demonstrable back door
into standards and shipped products~\cite{shumow2007dualec,checkoway2014dualec,checkoway2016juniper,bernstein2016dualec}.
Sony's PlayStation~3 used a constant signature nonce and lost its code-signing
key~\cite{fail0verflow2010ps3}. The list runs from smart cards to virtual machines to trusted
platform modules~\cite{garcia2008mifare,ristenpart2010vmreset,moghimi2020tpmfail}.

Each of these incidents has been analyzed in depth, and the architecture of a sound generator is
standardized: SP 800-90B specifies entropy sources, SP 800-90A deterministic generators, and
SP 800-90C their assembly into a complete random bit
generator~\cite{nist80090b,nist80090a,nist80090c}. It is also well established that statistical
test batteries cannot certify unpredictability~\cite{aumasson2024serious,nist80090b}. What is
missing is a joined account of the deployed record: for each failure, where in the assembled
system it occurred, which defenses could have stopped it, and whether those defenses existed when
the system shipped. Two loci recur in that record and sit outside the SP 800-90 architecture. One
is the consuming protocol, as in the reused signature nonces of the PlayStation~3 and of Android
wallets~\cite{fail0verflow2010ps3,michaelis2013randomly}. The other is the build and integration
layer that decides which generator is actually linked, as in the Debian patch of 2008, the
Juniper ScreenOS constant substitution, and
\coldcard{}~\cite{yilek2009debian,checkoway2016juniper,coinkite2026backgrounder}.

\paragraph{Contributions.}
This Systematization of Knowledge makes four contributions.
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*,topsep=2pt,itemsep=1pt]
\item \emph{A pipeline model} that adopts the SP 800-90 decomposition for stages \stage{1} to
\stage{5} and adds consumption (\stage{6}) and the build and integration surface
(\stage{$\ast$}), with an explicit mapping to the standards (Section~\ref{sec:taxonomy}).
\item \emph{A coded corpus} of twenty-eight deployed incidents from 1996 to 2026, chosen by
stated inclusion rules and coded on six attributes (Sections~\ref{sec:background}
and~\ref{sec:incidents}).
\item \emph{A remedy coverage matrix} that crosses every corpus incident with fourteen remedy
families under explicit grading rules and records whether a preventing remedy was available when
the system shipped. The matrix, its rules, and its generating script are released
(Sections~\ref{sec:remedies} and~\ref{sec:effectiveness}).
\item \emph{Findings for two audiences.} For researchers, open questions tied to the cells of the
matrix that no current remedy fills (Section~\ref{sec:discussion}). For implementers, a
prioritized checklist (Section~\ref{sec:checklist}).
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Main findings.}
Read by column, the matrix shows that statistical testing and certification prevent none of the
twenty-eight failures and flag only the two that are statistically visible, and that build-path
verification is the only remedy family that prevents a build and integration failure. Read by
row, it shows that 24 incidents have a preventing remedy in the catalog, and that in 22 of them
that remedy existed, at least in part, before the system shipped. Most of the record is
therefore a gap between available and deployed defenses. The remaining four incidents, a cache
side channel, two timing leaks, and a flawed prime generator, are prevented by no randomness
remedy at all. These counts describe our corpus, which was chosen to cover mechanisms rather
than as a census (Section~\ref{sec:selection}).

\paragraph{Scope.}
We study randomness failures in \emph{deployed} systems: shipped firmware, production libraries,
operating systems, and protocols, together with the standards that govern them. We include
failures whose consequence is a loss of confidentiality, integrity, or authentication traceable
to predictable, reused, or leaked random values, and we treat the theory of pseudorandom
generators only insofar as it explains deployed defenses.
